{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And this is how you shall make it.</b> HaShem commanded to make it according to this measure. And for the multiplicity of species that he would need to bring inside it, it was exceedingly small, for he would need to bring there unclean and clean beasts and birds, and small creatures together with large ones, two of each; and there are large creatures such as elephants and oryx. By nature, fifty arks like this one would have been too small to contain all these species. But the matter was undeniably a great miracle, and the little held the much." "<b>And perhaps</b> you will ask here: since the matter was a miracle, why did He command to make the ark from a specified wood, and in specified dimensions in length, breadth, and height, and to make three stories — lower, second, and third — when the Holy One, blessed be He, could have saved them without an ark, and they could have gone upon the face of the waters, or He could have made them fly through the air, for He is omnipotent and nothing is withheld from Him? And the answer to this is that it is the way of the Torah to command a person to do all he can by way of nature, and whatever nature lacks in these things, the miracle completes. Along these lines you will find this in all the miracles in the Torah, if you examine them: for even though they are celebrated miracles, there is in them an element of nature in part. And so it is with the matter of the Torah's command that the men of war go forth armed for battle. Likewise the verse that said (Joshua 8:2): ״שים לך אורב לעיר מאחריה״. And since all the affairs of Israel's wars were by way of great miracles, what need was there for an ambush in the manner of the nations, whose wars proceed according to nature? Rather, they were certainly meant to conduct themselves by way of nature, and HaShem would complete what was lacking. Therefore the commandment in the Torah comes to regulate the battle formations and many things like these. And there is yet another reason in this matter, mentioned by our Rabbis of blessed memory: through the making of the ark, in which he would toil constantly all day long in its length and its breadth, the people would see and speak of it, and they would turn back and be roused to return in repentance from their evil way; and if they did not return, they are wicked men of little faith, worthy that the attribute of judgment should come upon them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:51.608734902Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}